## Abstract

Closed commercial sports-analytics stacks often require proprietary camera rigs and budgets beyond smaller clubs and independent coaches. This bachelor thesis presents **TacticEYE2**, an open, engineerable pipeline that analyses **standard broadcast football video** to support coaching staff with near–real-time statistics and tactical cues. The system combines **YOLO** detectors (with domain fine-tuning), **Re-ID** tracking, **team** clustering from kit appearance, deterministic **ball possession** and **pass** estimation, optional **camera–pitch homography** via a dedicated **field keypoint** model, **heatmap** accumulation per team, and a **heuristic event-prediction engine** (YAML-tuned weights and cooldowns) that surfaces short-horizon risk signals—optionally phrased in Spanish via an LLM **without** deriving probabilities from generative text. A **FastAPI** web front-end streams progress through **WebSockets**; **Docker** and **GCP Cloud Build / Cloud Run** enable reproducible deployment.

The manuscript aligns objectives with the approved **anteproyecto**, maps each goal to implemented modules, and states honest gaps (e.g., full coach-facing strategic recommendations beyond heuristic alerts).

**Keywords:** computer vision, deep learning, YOLO, re-identification, homography, sports analytics, decision support, real-time systems, FastAPI, Docker, Google Cloud Run.

